{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"08","chapter_title":"Digital Citizenship and AI Readiness","title":"Module 1 Readiness Studio","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting module 1 readiness studio to module 1 readiness map, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-01","statement":"Audit a single artifact against all four Module 1 skills at once"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-02","statement":"Mark each skill honestly as pass or gap based on what the artifact actually shows"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-03","statement":"Resist the urge to check every box; flag real gaps instead of hiding them"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-04","statement":"Convert each gap into a specific, targeted practice step"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness","cat100_supplement_module_1_readiness_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/01/07"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_calibration","cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_recheck"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_recheck"],"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Module 1 Readiness Studio\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Module 1 Readiness Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/01-08-module-1-readiness-studio.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Module 1 Readiness Studio: Module 1 Readiness Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThis studio is where the separate skills of Module 1 stop being separate. A real artifact, like a profile slide or a short project page, demands all of them at once: the claims must be truthful (1.1), any AI help must be disclosed (1.2 to 1.4), the design must be accessible (1.6), and the accounts behind it must be safe (1.5). The readiness check is an honest self-audit that asks, for one shared artifact, which skills you can actually demonstrate and which still have a gap.\n\nThe work product is a readiness map: each Module 1 skill checked against the same artifact, marked pass or gap, with the gaps flagged for targeted practice. The point of the studio is not to declare yourself finished; it is to find the one or two things that are not yet ready before they cost you on a graded assignment.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Audit a single artifact against all four Module 1 skills at once.\n- Mark each skill honestly as pass or gap based on what the artifact actually shows.\n- Resist the urge to check every box; flag real gaps instead of hiding them.\n- Convert each gap into a specific, targeted practice step.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nRosa is about to submit her class profile slide and move to Module 2. She runs the readiness check across all four skills. Her resume bullet is truthful, her AI-use note is present, and her slide has alt text and a real heading. But when she checks privacy, she realizes two-factor authentication is not yet on for her school email. Three skills pass; one has a real gap.\n\nThe tempting move is to mark privacy \"good enough\" and submit. The studio's lesson is the opposite: flag the gap, enable 2FA, and re-check that one row. An honest map with one flagged gap is more useful than a clean map that hides a real weakness.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA readiness check is integration, not repetition. The earlier sections taught each skill in isolation; the studio tests whether they hold together on one artifact under one set of eyes. The instrument is a single table that lists each skill, the exact thing you inspect on the artifact, and an honest status.\n\n| Skill (from this module) | What you inspect | Honest status |\n|--------------------------|------------------|---------------|\n| Truthful evidence (1.1) | Does each claim survive a reference check? (Pick one number-bearing bullet and confirm you could point to where the number came from if a grader asked.) | Pass / Gap |\n| AI disclosure (1.2 to 1.4) | Is AI use named, with a rejection logged? | Pass / Gap |\n| Accessibility (1.6) | Alt text, headings, color, contrast | Pass / Gap |\n| Privacy (1.5) | Unique password, 2FA, no exposed data | Pass / Gap |\n\nTwo ideas make the check work. First, you inspect the actual artifact, not your memory of it: open the slide, read the alt text, look at the disclosure, check the 2FA setting. Memory inflates readiness; inspection corrects it. Second, a flagged gap is a feature, not a failure. The whole value of a self-audit is finding the gap while you still have time to fix it; a check that always returns \"all pass\" is performing readiness rather than measuring it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A learner marks all four readiness rows pass in under a minute without opening the slide, the disclosure, or the account settings. What is the most accurate read of that map?\" explanation=\"A readiness check earns its value by surfacing the weak row before a grader does. An all-pass map produced from memory rather than inspection is performing readiness, not measuring it; the honest move is to open each artifact and mark gaps where the evidence is not actually present.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is strong: a clean all-pass map is the goal\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is suspect: statuses came from memory, not inspection, so re-run each row against the actual artifact\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is fine as long as the learner feels confident\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is strong because it was completed quickly\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first pass at a readiness check often hides the real state behind optimism:\n\n> Everything looks ready.\n\nThat sentence is too thin because it names no skill and reflects no inspection. It is a feeling, not an audit. A strong readiness map runs each skill against the artifact and reports the true status, including any gap.\n\n### Worked Example: running the Module 1 readiness check, step by step\n\nTest all four module skills against one shared profile slide before moving to Module 2.\n\n| Skill (from this module) | What you check on the slide | Status |\n|--------------------------|-----------------------------|--------|\n| Truthful evidence (1.1) | Bullet reads \"Co-led a 4-person team\" and is accurate | Pass |\n| AI disclosure (1.2 to 1.4) | A note lists the AI brainstorm and the rejected thesis rewrite | Pass |\n| Accessibility (1.6) | The slide image has alt text and uses a real heading style | Pass |\n| Privacy (1.5) | Two-factor authentication is on; no student ID shown | Gap: 2FA not yet enabled |\n\n1. **Run each row honestly**, opening the artifact rather than assuming.\n2. **Flag the gap, do not hide it.** Privacy is not ready: 2FA is off.\n3. **Request targeted practice** on enabling two-factor authentication, then re-check that one row.\n\nResult: three skills confirmed against real evidence and one gap flagged for practice. That honest readiness map is more useful than checking every box at once.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict each move as you run the Module 1 readiness check\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You are auditing the privacy row. What is the correct way to set its status?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Mark pass because you usually keep accounts safe\",\"Open the account settings, check whether 2FA is actually on, and mark the status from what you see\",\"Average it with the other rows\",\"Skip it; three of four is enough\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"You inspect the actual artifact, not your memory. Opening the settings shows 2FA is off, so the honest status is gap, even though the other rows pass.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Privacy comes back as a gap: 2FA is not enabled. What is the right next move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Mark it good enough and submit\",\"Flag the gap and write the specific fix: enable a passkey or app-based 2FA on the school email\",\"Hide the row so the map looks clean\",\"Remove the privacy row entirely\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A flagged gap is the point of the audit. You name the concrete fix while you still have time, instead of letting a grader find it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"After you enable 2FA, can you mark the privacy row pass?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, immediately, because you intended to fix it\",\"Not until you re-inspect the setting and confirm 2FA is actually on\",\"Yes, once the other three rows pass\",\"No, gaps can never become pass\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The loop is inspect, fix, re-inspect. The row is not pass until you confirm the fix on the actual artifact; skipping re-inspection leaves you believing a problem is solved when it may not be.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final map: three rows confirmed by inspection, one gap flagged with a concrete fix, then re-checked. An honest map with a well-handled gap shows more skill than a flawless map that is not true.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nTo lock the method in, order the steps of one honest readiness pass.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Open the actual artifact for the row you are checking\",\"Mark pass only if the evidence is present, otherwise mark gap\",\"Write a specific fix for every flagged gap\",\"Apply the fix to the gap\",\"Re-inspect that one row and confirm before marking it pass\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor each skill, mark pass only if you just inspected the artifact and the evidence is actually there; otherwise mark gap and write the specific fix. Do not average the skills into a single \"ready\" feeling, because one real gap (an exposed account, a false claim) can undermine the whole artifact regardless of how strong the other rows are. The honest call on the weakest row is more important than the count of passing rows.\n\n1. **Inspection check:** Did you open the artifact for each row, or rely on memory?\n2. **Gap check:** Is every gap flagged with a specific fix rather than smoothed over?\n3. **Re-check loop:** After fixing a gap, did you re-run that one row to confirm the fix?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is the all-pass reflex: marking every row \"good\" because admitting a gap feels like admitting failure. But a readiness check that never finds a gap is useless; its entire purpose is to surface the weak row before a grader does. A map with one honest gap shows more skill than a flawless map that is not true.\n\nA second misreading is fixing a gap and forgetting to re-check it. If privacy was a gap and you enable 2FA, the row is not \"pass\" until you confirm 2FA is actually on. The loop is inspect, fix, re-inspect; skipping the final inspection leaves you believing a problem is solved when it may not be.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nImagine a peer-review pairing in the studio: you audit a classmate's project page and they audit yours, each using the four-row table. You open their page and check the resume line against plausibility, look for an AI-use note, test the images for alt text and the text for contrast, and ask whether any private data is exposed. You find the page passes three rows but shows a phone number in a contact image with no indication it was meant to be public, so you flag a privacy gap. They find that one of your headings is just bold text, so they flag an accessibility gap.\n\nThe walkthrough shows why the studio uses a shared, inspectable table: it makes the audit transferable between people. Because the rows name exactly what to inspect, a classmate can run the same check you would, and the gaps they flag are specific enough to fix. Readiness becomes a shared, evidence-based judgment rather than each person's private sense that their work is \"probably fine.\"\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nWhen you read a readiness map, look for whether the statuses came from inspection and whether the gaps are honest. A map that is all \"pass\" with no inspection notes is suspect; a map that flags a gap and names the fix shows a learner who is actually auditing rather than performing. The presence of a well-handled gap is a sign of strength, not weakness.\n\nA strong reader of a readiness map can answer four questions:\n\n1. Was each row checked against the actual artifact?\n2. Is every status honest, including the gaps?\n3. Does each gap have a specific, targeted fix?\n4. Were fixed gaps re-checked before being marked pass?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour readiness map is strong when each row's status is backed by an inspection a reviewer could repeat, and every gap carries a concrete next step. The earlier layers are the per-skill checks from sections 1.1 through 1.7; the studio's added layer is honest integration. If you are unsure whether a row passes, mark it a gap and ask BigAL to help you inspect that specific element rather than asking it to declare you ready.\n\nA useful note would read: \"Across my profile slide, truthful evidence, AI disclosure, and accessibility pass on inspection. Privacy is a gap because 2FA is off; my next step is to enable a passkey, or app-based 2FA where passkeys are not offered (the current standard, since SMS codes are the weakest factor), on my school email and re-check that row before submitting.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the readiness-check habit into any multi-requirement deliverable later in college or work: a lab report, a portfolio submission, a job application packet. The structure transfers directly. List the requirements as rows, name the exact thing to inspect for each, open the actual artifact, mark pass or gap honestly, and convert gaps into specific fixes before you submit. The constraint changes (the requirements come from a rubric or a job posting rather than this module), but the method does not: integration through honest, repeatable inspection. A student who has practiced flagging their own gaps here will, later, catch the missing requirement in a real submission instead of discovering it in the grade.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the module 1 readiness map packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-08-module-1-readiness-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"module 1 readiness map\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your readiness map with at least one honest gap. The revised version should show the gap fixed and the row re-checked. The trace should explain how you confirmed the fix.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare an all-pass map with an inspected map that flags a real gap.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the readiness-map method to a future multi-requirement deliverable.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes which row you re-check.\n\nBigAL is the studio's built-in assistant. If you bring an outside general-purpose assistant into an inspection step, name it the way you would in a disclosure: a current assistant such as ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini in their 2026 versions. Model versions change quickly, so record which one you used and when. Either way, the AI helps you inspect; it does not declare you ready.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are running a readiness check for CAT 100 01.08. A peer comment notes that every row is marked pass without any inspection notes. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show each status came from inspecting the artifact.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_module_1_readiness_studio\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_module_1_readiness_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your readiness map inspects each Module 1 skill against the artifact, reports honest statuses including gaps, and converts every gap into a specific fix you re-checked.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_1_readiness_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_1_readiness_studio","cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"],"stem":"The Module 1 readiness checklist asks you to confirm \"my resume bullets show measurable results.\" How should you check this box honestly?","options":["Check it because you finished the resume section.","Open your resume, confirm each bullet has an action verb plus a number or outcome, and only then check the box.","Check every box at once to save time.","Leave it unchecked since checklists do not matter."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A readiness check is a real test, not a formality. Verify the artifact meets the standard before marking it complete.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_checklist_is_busywork","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_1_readiness_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_1_readiness_studio"],"stem":"You are finalizing one shared profile slide for the studio. Which approach reflects that the module's skills work together?","options":["Make it look polished and ignore the other module skills.","Check that it has truthful evidence, a disclosed AI-use note, alt text on its image, and no exposed private data, all at once.","Apply only the resume skill, since that is the main one.","Run a single AI pass and assume it handled everything."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The module's skills combine in one artifact. Readiness means evidence, AI disclosure, accessibility, and privacy are all handled together.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_skills_dont_connect","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You ask the studio's AI, \"Am I ready for Module 2?\" and it replies \"Yes, you are ready.\" How should you treat this?","options":["Accept it and move on; the studio decided.","Use it as a prompt to inspect your own artifacts against each target, since you confirm readiness, not the tool.","Ask three more times until you get a different answer.","Skip checking your artifacts because the AI is confident."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The studio supports your self-assessment but does not replace it. You verify readiness by checking your own evidence against the standards.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_studio_replaces_judgment","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Going through the checklist, you find your privacy skill is still shaky: you have not enabled two-factor authentication. What is the most useful thing to record?","options":["Mark privacy as complete so the module looks finished.","Flag privacy as a gap and request targeted practice on enabling two-factor authentication.","Hide the gap and hope it does not come up later.","Delete the privacy item from the checklist."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An honestly flagged gap routes you to the right practice. A false all-clear hides exactly the skill you most need to build.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_gaps_left_unmarked","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_recheck","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Your readiness checklist flagged 'no two-factor authentication' as a gap. You just walked through your account's 2FA setup screens. Can you mark the privacy row 'pass'?","options":["Yes; you performed the fix, so the gap is closed.","Not yet: re-open the account's security settings and confirm 2FA is actually showing as enabled before the row earns its pass.","Yes, as long as you note the date you made the change.","No; privacy rows can never be marked pass."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The distinctive failure of checklist work is fixing a gap and forgetting to re-inspect it. Setup flows fail silently (an unconfirmed code, an unsaved toggle), so the loop closes only when the artifact itself shows the fixed state. Inspect, fix, re-inspect.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_fix_without_recheck","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-08-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_checklist_is_busywork","pattern":"readiness_check_seen_as_busywork","description":"Treating the module readiness checklist as a formality rather than a real self-check before moving on","trigger":"learner marks every item complete without testing whether each skill works","feedback":"Use the checklist to actually test each skill: open the artifact and confirm it meets the standard before checking the box.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_skills_dont_connect","pattern":"module_skills_treated_as_isolated","description":"Treating the module's skills as separate boxes rather than parts of one digital readiness practice","trigger":"learner applies one skill without considering related ones in the same artifact","feedback":"Skills combine: a resume artifact also needs truthful evidence, AI disclosure, accessibility, and privacy care at once.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_studio_replaces_judgment","pattern":"ai_studio_replaces_self_assessment","description":"Letting the AI studio decide readiness instead of judging your own evidence against the standard","trigger":"learner asks the tool whether they are ready and accepts the verdict without checking artifacts","feedback":"The studio supports your self-check; you confirm readiness by inspecting your own artifacts against each target.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_gaps_left_unmarked","pattern":"weak_areas_hidden_not_flagged","description":"Hiding weak areas to look finished instead of flagging gaps for targeted practice","trigger":"learner reports full readiness while one skill is still shaky","feedback":"Mark the gap honestly and request practice on that specific skill; a flagged weakness is more useful than a false all-clear.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_8_module_1_readiness_studio_fix_without_recheck","pattern":"gap_marked_pass_without_reinspection","description":"Marking a checklist row 'pass' as soon as a fix is intended or applied, without re-inspecting the actual artifact to confirm the fix took effect.","trigger":"learner flips a flagged row to pass right after making a change, with no re-check on the artifact itself","feedback":"A fix is a claim until you re-inspect. Re-open the setting, the page, or the document and confirm the gap is actually closed on the artifact; only then does the row earn its pass. Inspect, fix, re-inspect is one loop, not two steps.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"531f15319acc6c88d3cbd5273bf1c9c4d06c58b7a73f5ce0194554fdc7448c71","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}